DEV ROSA Cluster Upgrade – Observations, Issues and Resolutions

| Item                              | Details                                                                                                                         |
| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Environment**                   | DEV                                                                                                                             |
| **Platform**                      | Red Hat OpenShift Service on AWS (ROSA)                                                                                         |
| **Upgrade**                       | OpenShift `4.19.40` → `4.20.33`                                                                                                 |
| **Upgrade Type**                  | Minor version upgrade                                                                                                           |
| **Primary Issue**                 | Machine Config Pools became degraded because nodes could not complete drain operations                                          |
| **Primary Root Cause Identified** | Stale Dynatrace OneAgent DaemonSet pods stuck in deletion/termination state                                                     |
| **Additional Observations**       | Authentication/OpenShift API degradation, kubelet version skew during rollout, OLM upgradeability warning for OpenShift Logging |
| **Overall Status**                | Upgrade progressing after remediation; final cluster health validation required after all MCP/node updates complete             |


2. Executive Summary

During the DEV ROSA upgrade from OpenShift 4.19.40 to 4.20.33, the upgrade became stalled while the Machine Config Operator (MCO) attempted to update control-plane and worker nodes.

Both the master and worker MachineConfigPools entered a Degraded state because MCO was unable to drain specific nodes within its one-hour drain timeout.

Initial affected nodes were:

Master:
ip-10-64-34-146.ec2.internal

Worker:
ip-10-64-34-142.ec2.internal

Investigation of the MCO logs identified stale Dynatrace OneAgent pods as the drain blocker. The affected Dynatrace pods were in Succeeded/Completed state but had been stuck with a deletion timestamp since:

2026-08-07T19:24:11Z

For example:

dynakube-oneagent-dsw22
dynakube-oneagent-fpl2x

The pods were owned by:

DaemonSet/dynakube-oneagent

and did not contain finalizers.

MCO repeatedly attempted to evict the stale pods and eventually timed out waiting for their termination. Force-removing the already-terminating stale pod objects allowed the affected nodes to continue their MachineConfig update.

Following remediation, both initially affected nodes successfully advanced from kubelet v1.32.13 to v1.33.13.

Further investigation identified the same stale Dynatrace pod condition across additional nodes, indicating a broader Dynatrace OneAgent/CSI cleanup issue rather than isolated node failures.

3. Initial Upgrade Symptoms

The OpenShift console showed the cluster continuing to report:

This cluster is updating from 4.19.40 to 4.20.33

Several ClusterOperators displayed degraded or cannot-update conditions.

Cluster Operator	Observed Status	Observation
| Cluster Operator             | Observed Status | Observation                                                 |
| ---------------------------- | --------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| `authentication`             | Degraded        | 1 of 3 requested API/OAuth instances unavailable            |
| `openshift-apiserver`        | Degraded        | 1 of 3 requested instances unavailable                      |
| `kube-apiserver`             | Cannot update   | Multiple nodes still running an older kubelet minor version |
| `machine-config`             | Cannot update   | One or more MachineConfigPools degraded                     |
| `operator-lifecycle-manager` | Cannot update   | Logging 6.3.4 prevents upgrade to OCP 4.21 or later         |
| Other upgraded operators     | Available       | Already reporting 4.20.33                                   |


The primary issue preventing the node rollout from completing was the MachineConfigPool degradation.

4. Issue 1 – Master MachineConfigPool Drain Failure
Observation

The master MCP reported:

Updated:    False
Updating:   True
Degraded:   True

The detailed condition showed:

Node ip-10-64-34-146.ec2.internal is reporting:
"failed to drain node: ip-10-64-34-146.ec2.internal after 1 hour.
Please see machine-config-controller logs for more information"

The master pool consisted of:

Machine Count:            3
Degraded Machine Count:   1
Unavailable Machine Count: 1
Investigation

Workloads on the affected master were inspected:

oc get pods -A \
  --field-selector spec.nodeName=ip-10-64-34-146.ec2.internal \
  -o wide

The control-plane components themselves were running:

etcd
kube-apiserver
kube-controller-manager
kube-scheduler

A Dynatrace pod was also identified:

dynatrace/dynakube-oneagent-dsw22
STATUS: Completed

MCO logs showed repeated attempts to evict this pod and subsequent timeout while waiting for the pod to terminate.

Root Cause

Inspection showed:

Pod:       dynakube-oneagent-dsw22
Phase:     Succeeded
Deleting:  2026-08-07T19:24:11Z
Owner:     DaemonSet/dynakube-oneagent
Finalizer: None

The pod had therefore already been marked for deletion for an extended period but remained present.

The pod also used the Dynatrace CSI driver:

Driver: csi.oneagent.dynatrace.com
mode=host

MCO could not complete the node drain because it continued waiting for this stale pod to terminate.

Resolution

A normal pod deletion was attempted first but the pod remained stuck.

The already-terminating stale pod was subsequently force removed:

oc delete pod dynakube-oneagent-dsw22 \
  -n dynatrace \
  --grace-period=0 \
  --force

Verification:

oc get pod dynakube-oneagent-dsw22 -n dynatrace

Result:

Error from server (NotFound)
Result

After removing the stale Dynatrace pod:

master DEGRADED: True → False

and node:

ip-10-64-34-146.ec2.internal

successfully progressed from:

kubelet v1.32.13

to:

kubelet v1.33.13

This confirmed that the stale Dynatrace pod was blocking the MCO drain/update process.

5. Issue 2 – Worker MachineConfigPool Drain Failure
Observation

The worker MCP independently reported:

Node ip-10-64-34-142.ec2.internal is reporting:
"failed to drain node: ip-10-64-34-142.ec2.internal after 1 hour"

Initial worker status included:

Machine Count:           8
Degraded Machine Count:  1
Updated Machine Count:   2
Investigation

Pods on the affected worker were inspected:

oc get pods -A \
  --field-selector spec.nodeName=ip-10-64-34-142.ec2.internal \
  -o wide

Another stale Dynatrace pod was identified:

dynakube-oneagent-fpl2x
0/1
Completed

Inspection showed exactly the same condition as the master:

Phase:     Succeeded
Deleting:  2026-08-07T19:24:11Z
Finalizer: None
Owner:     DaemonSet/dynakube-oneagent

MCO logs confirmed the drain controller was attempting to evict:

dynatrace/dynakube-oneagent-fpl2x

and the drain eventually exceeded the one-hour timeout.

Resolution

The stale pod was force removed:

oc delete pod dynakube-oneagent-fpl2x \
  -n dynatrace \
  --grace-period=0 \
  --force

Verification returned:

Error from server (NotFound):
pods "dynakube-oneagent-fpl2x" not found
Result

Worker:

ip-10-64-34-142.ec2.internal

subsequently progressed to:

kubelet v1.33.13

confirming the same Dynatrace stale-pod condition was affecting the worker rollout.

6. Issue 3 – Cluster-Wide Stale Dynatrace OneAgent Pods

Further investigation identified that the problem was not limited to the two initially degraded nodes.

The following Dynatrace pods were also found in Succeeded state with the same deletion timestamp:

dynakube-oneagent-4d5v9  -> ip-10-64-34-133
dynakube-oneagent-b55s8  -> ip-10-64-34-132
dynakube-oneagent-g42fj  -> ip-10-64-34-152
dynakube-oneagent-gnfpt  -> ip-10-64-34-153
dynakube-oneagent-mtk9w  -> ip-10-64-34-145
dynakube-oneagent-qbkrw  -> ip-10-64-34-150
dynakube-oneagent-v6rxq  -> ip-10-64-34-134

All reported:

Phase:    Succeeded
Deleting: 2026-08-07T19:24:11Z

This is an important observation because MCO processes nodes incrementally. As MCO reached another node, the stale Dynatrace pod on that node could cause another drain timeout.

For example, MCO subsequently attempted:

evicting pod dynatrace/dynakube-oneagent-qbkrw
Resolution approach

After confirming that remaining affected pods had the same characteristics—Succeeded, already deleting, DaemonSet-owned, and no finalizers—the stale pod objects could be cleaned up to prevent repeated one-hour drain failures.

This should be limited specifically to the confirmed stale/terminating Dynatrace pods and not used as a general cleanup of healthy Dynatrace workloads.

7. Issue 4 – Kubelet Version Skew During Upgrade

Initially:

9 nodes -> v1.32.13
2 nodes -> v1.33.13

The OpenShift console reported:

KubeletMinorVersionUpgradeable:
Kubelet minor versions on 9 nodes will not be supported
in the next OpenShift minor version upgrade.

After clearing the stale Dynatrace pods on 146 and 142, the count decreased from:

9 → 7

Later node status showed:

v1.33.13:
142
144
146
151

v1.32.13:
132
133
134
145
150
152
153

This demonstrated that the kubelet warning was largely a symptom of the incomplete MachineConfig/node rollout, rather than something requiring manual kubelet installation.

Resolution

No manual kubelet upgrade was performed.

The correct approach was to allow MCO to update each node normally after removing the drain blocker.

Expected final state:

All nodes -> v1.33.13
8. Issue 5 – Authentication and OpenShift API Server Degraded

During the upgrade, the console reported:

authentication
APIServerDeploymentDegraded:
1 of 3 requested instances are unavailable

and:

OAuthServerDeploymentDegraded:
1 of 3 requested instances are unavailable

openshift-apiserver similarly reported:

1 of 3 requested instances are unavailable
Observation

Several control-plane PodDisruptionBudgets showed:

ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS = 0

including:

openshift-apiserver-pdb
etcd-guard-pdb
kube-apiserver-guard-pdb
kube-controller-manager-guard-pdb
openshift-kube-scheduler-guard-pdb
oauth-apiserver-pdb
Decision

The OpenShift-managed PDBs were not modified.

Because the control plane was already partially degraded, bypassing PDB protection or force-draining a master could have reduced control-plane availability further.

Investigation instead focused on the actual MCO drain logs, which identified the Dynatrace pods as the concrete drain blocker.

Follow-up

After MCP completion, validate:

oc get co authentication openshift-apiserver kube-apiserver

Expected:

AVAILABLE=True
PROGRESSING=False
DEGRADED=False

If degradation remains after the MCP/node rollout finishes, investigate these operators separately.

9. Issue 6 – OpenShift Logging / OLM Warning

The console displayed:

operator-lifecycle-manager
Cannot update

with:

ClusterServiceVersions blocking minor version upgrades
to 4.21.0 or higher:

maximum supported OCP version for
openshift-logging/cluster-logging.v6.3.4 is 4.20

Installed Logging Operator:

cluster-logging.v6.3.4
Phase: Succeeded

Subscription:

NAME              PACKAGE           SOURCE             CHANNEL
cluster-logging   cluster-logging   redhat-operators   stable-6.1
Assessment

This message does not block the current 4.19 → 4.20 upgrade.

It indicates that:

4.19 → 4.20    Supported
4.20 → 4.21+   Blocked by current Logging CSV compatibility
Resolution

No change was made to the Logging Operator during the ongoing 4.20 upgrade.

The Logging subscription/operator compatibility should be reviewed after 4.20.33 is fully stable and before any future 4.21 upgrade.

10. Root Cause Summary
Primary root cause

The ROSA upgrade stalled because Dynatrace OneAgent DaemonSet pods were stuck in a stale deletion state.

The affected objects had:

Status:             Succeeded/Completed
Deletion Timestamp: 2026-08-07T19:24:11Z
Finalizers:         None
Owner:              DaemonSet/dynakube-oneagent
CSI Driver:         csi.oneagent.dynatrace.com

During the OpenShift upgrade, MCO attempted to drain nodes. The drain process attempted to evict these stale pods but could not observe their termination, eventually reaching the MCO one-hour drain timeout.

This resulted in:

Node drain failure
        ↓
NodeDegraded=True
        ↓
MCP Degraded=True
        ↓
Machine Config rollout stalled
        ↓
Nodes remained on kubelet v1.32.13
        ↓
Cluster upgrade remained incomplete

Removal of the stale pod objects allowed affected nodes to resume updating.

11. Actions Taken
| Action                                         | Result                                                                        |
| ---------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Reviewed `oc describe mcp`                     | Identified master `146` and worker `142` drain failures                       |
| Reviewed workloads on affected nodes           | Identified stale Dynatrace OneAgent pods                                      |
| Reviewed PDBs                                  | Several control-plane PDBs had zero disruptions; left unchanged               |
| Reviewed MCO controller logs                   | Confirmed MCO was repeatedly waiting on Dynatrace pod eviction/termination    |
| Inspected Dynatrace pod metadata               | Confirmed `Succeeded`, old deletion timestamp, DaemonSet owner, no finalizers |
| Attempted normal pod deletion                  | Pod remained stuck                                                            |
| Force-deleted stale `dynakube-oneagent-dsw22`  | Master drain resumed                                                          |
| Force-deleted stale `dynakube-oneagent-fpl2x`  | Worker `142` subsequently updated                                             |
| Verified kubelet versions                      | `142` and `146` advanced from `v1.32.13` → `v1.33.13`                         |
| Investigated remaining Dynatrace pods          | Found same stale condition across seven additional nodes                      |
| Left control-plane PDBs unchanged              | Avoided unnecessary control-plane availability risk                           |
| Left Logging Operator unchanged during upgrade | OLM warning applies to future 4.21 upgrade                                    |

