13. Validation / Exit Criteria

The DEV upgrade should not be considered fully completed until all of the following are satisfied.

oc get clusterversion

Expected:

VERSION    AVAILABLE   PROGRESSING
4.20.33    True        False

Check MCPs:

oc get mcp master worker

Expected:

NAME     UPDATED   UPDATING   DEGRADED
master   True      False      False
worker   True      False      False

Expected counts:

master:
Ready = 3
Updated = 3
Degraded = 0

worker:
Ready = 8
Updated = 8
Degraded = 0

Verify kubelets:

oc get nodes \
  -o custom-columns='NAME:.metadata.name,KUBELET:.status.nodeInfo.kubeletVersion'

Expected:

All nodes: v1.33.13

Finally:

oc get co

Confirm that no operator related to the current upgrade remains unexpectedly:

AVAILABLE=False
DEGRADED=True
PROGRESSING=True
14. Follow-Up / Preventive Actions

Dynatrace requires a separate follow-up investigation. Multiple OneAgent pods across different nodes had exactly the same 2026-08-07T19:24:11Z deletion timestamp and remained present for more than two weeks. This indicates a broader lifecycle/cleanup problem.

The following should be reviewed before the next DEV maintenance window:

Dynatrace Operator version and compatibility with the installed OpenShift releases.
Dynatrace CSI driver health and logs.
Why dynakube-oneagent DaemonSet pods remained after receiving a deletion timestamp.
Whether this condition exists in QA/PROD before their OpenShift upgrades.
OpenShift Logging Operator compatibility/subscription should be corrected before a future 4.20 → 4.21 upgrade.
Add a pre-upgrade check for pods stuck in Terminating for an extended period.

A useful pre-upgrade check is:

oc get pods -A \
  -o custom-columns='NAMESPACE:.metadata.namespace,NAME:.metadata.name,PHASE:.status.phase,NODE:.spec.nodeName,DELETING:.metadata.deletionTimestamp' \
  | grep -v '<none>'

This can identify workloads already marked for deletion before MCO begins draining nodes.

15. Lessons Learned

The main operational takeaway is that MCP Degraded during an OpenShift upgrade should be investigated from the MCO drain error before modifying PDBs or manually manipulating nodes.

In this incident, several control-plane PDBs initially appeared suspicious because ALLOWED DISRUPTIONS=0, but they were functioning as intended. The MCO logs exposed the actual blocker: stale Dynatrace pods.

Similarly, the kubelet version warning was not an independent kubelet problem. It reflected nodes that MCO had not yet been able to update.

The remediation sequence that proved effective was:

MCP Degraded
      ↓
Identify affected node
      ↓
Inspect MCO drain logs
      ↓
Identify non-terminating Dynatrace pod
      ↓
Verify:
  Succeeded
  already deleting
  no finalizer
  DaemonSet-owned
      ↓
Remove stale pod object
      ↓
Allow MCO to retry automatically
      ↓
Node updates/reboots
      ↓
Kubelet 1.32.13 → 1.33.13
      ↓
MCP progresses

No manual kubelet upgrade, PDB bypass, MCP patch, or manual master drain was required.

DEV incident status

Current classification: Remediation in progress / upgrade progressing.

Confirmed primary issue: stale Dynatrace OneAgent pods preventing MCO node drains.

Immediate next objective: allow all remaining nodes to reach v1.33.13, both MCPs to become Updated=True / Updating=False / Degraded=False, and then perform final ClusterOperator and ClusterVersion validation.
